{"authored" [{"question" "q-7619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/2bc4b82c6cfae63026f221dd49cdc74a9ce3884443e933cae02c36f6d732707e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-7619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,4),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=1,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-7619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0e36ba8271e2e827a6c40d37f976e956cdd830dd7da7a