c1309b5495eae92591d4c9098e9ecbf19cc95923300a"} {"question" "q-7619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-7619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{3}=1"] ["math" "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}=1"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{3}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{9}+(y-2)^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-7619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-16548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-16548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=3,$ so the center is located at $(-4,3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-16548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d652104e81de10b1e9e2c1309b5495eae92591d4c9098e9ecbf19cc95923300a"} {"question" "q-16548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-16548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$2$ and $\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $2$"] ["text" "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-16548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-72252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/assets/28079ead5ebb4649900653172d11c25f3ff489c6b34772094a1ba947c18ef3e1.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-72252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,-2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072906/state.json" "file_sha256" "a38a031f8a8be1afce8983ebd6ad216ed3c08b4e5a063bdd8f1ca3ffe349aad3"} {"question" "q-72252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/eas